{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: the R14 usage-report script (child 00abd5aa). Head 1c89fa19d126e2eb3ed8f77b9430969442ed35f4, tree bd79f5f6, branch request/security-r14-usage-20261027. Main d73b53c9 is the merge base. The delta is 13 paths, 2,185 insertions and 1 deletion. Review request 54c62b5e, checker promise 190f63c0, request 00abd5aa, builder promise fe24c679, ruling c0832d1f. Primary artifact 672f4e54 (the PD-2 note), first of 16. gs inspect shows it resting on promise fe24c679, request 00abd5aa and ruling c0832d1f.\n\nRead order: the note in full; 00abd5aa and c0832d1f; scripts/usage-report.mjs in full; the security-settings.mjs export, the test-owner line and docs/security/settings.md; docs/usage-report.md; the test file and fixtures; the raw control records; the gate record; the manifest and the sealed directory. I did not run any suite again.\n\nAuthority chain:\n1. Credential. The only credential is the operator's token. The script reads it from bounded stdin and nothing else: at most 4,096 visible ASCII bytes plus one LF or CRLF, a 10-second deadline, and a refusal of a terminal. It reads no environment (no process.env), has no token flag (a --token argument is refused, and the refusal does not echo it), and reads no token file. The token is sent only in the Authorization header of one POST to the account's analytics_engine/sql endpoint. That POST has no retry and no redirect, and runs under a 30-second deadline with an 8 MiB and 65,536-chunk bound.\n2. Worker identity. Deployment labels come from an operator mapping whose real path must lie outside the worktree and the common Git repository; symlinks are resolved first. The report names the mapping only as operator-declared, with its SHA-256 and a count of labels. It does not authenticate Worker identity, and the note and the docs page say so.\n3. Data. The SQL interpolates only two validated integers for an exact 30-day half-open window, and selects no request, trace, scope, body or signature field.\n\nRequest conditions (00abd5aa, c0832d1f):\n1. Token non-disclosure. The report emits no provider-supplied string: places must be in the metrics catalog, results, reasons and executions are closed sets, and labels are dropped. Provider error text is never emitted. As a last barrier, a report containing the token text is withheld (exit 1, no output). The original collision bug, where a fixed key could equal a TEST credential, is recorded as a failure before the repair. Control: disabling the barrier exposes the colliding TEST strings (\"actual CLI report output must omit every accepted TEST credential\").\n2. Unmappable Worker. A selected Worker missing from the mapping, or mapped only to \"unknown\", is marked refused with reason mapping-missing or mapping-unknown-label, and the run exits 2. A mapping that names a Worker outside the inventory is refused as input. Control: refused:false makes the run succeed (\"an unmappable configured Worker prevents success\", 0 against 2).\n3. Truncation. The SQL asks for 8,193 groups, and more than 8,192 refuses the response. rows must equal the data length, and a rows_before_limit_at_least hint above it refuses. A refused response gives query-incomplete, status unobservable, exit 1, and no zero claim. Control: admitting cap plus one returns 0 against 1.\n4. Status wording. A place is observed, zero-observed or unobservable. Zero-observed reads \"no observed use in returned points; sampling, loss, missing summaries and operator-declared aliases do not prove absence.\" absenceProven is always false. A mapped Worker with no points is unobservable (telemetry-missing), not zero. Individual routes, bindings, native platform versions and features are always unobservable, each with a fixed reason. Control: \"never used\" fails the wording assertion.\n5. All four controls ran on the final source (SHA-256 4860370b, equal to the head's script). Each passed node --check, failed exactly its named assertion, and restored the source. I read each raw output. The earlier round, before the response-envelope change, is kept separately.\n6. No live query in the gate, no Worker setting, no deployment. The recorded TEST emissions run the actual Metrics writer into a scripted sink. The SQL envelope is labelled as a separately written projection, not a provider recording.\n\nValidation:\n- The combined-tree ordinary gate at df115cd1 (tree e78d5a2c) passed: exit 0, clean before and after, Node 66, Counting 55, plain 2,859 passed and 1 skipped, Native 795 passed and 2 skipped, 0 unhandled. The note gives 909.9 s wall and 1,929.3 s child CPU (one run). The earlier gate on f9aca97f is kept as a prior-base result only.\n- After the gate only the note and the manifest change, so the documents-only rule applies.\n- The manifest verifies against the sealed recut directory: 160 files, 0 bad, nothing writable, SHA256SUMS all OK.\n- check-notes d73b53c9..1c89fa19 reports 0 warnings, and git diff --check is clean.\n- check-doc-links on an exported tree of the head: 712 relative links checked, 0 broken.\n\nArchitecture: sound for an operator script. It is read-only and offline except for the one query, and it reuses the served-route inventory and the settings parser; the only change there is the export of configOf. No Worker is imported or started, and it adds no dependency, setting, telemetry field or deployment.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding. One point for the planner: the response envelope and column types are local rules, not a recording of the hosted API. Any unknown envelope field or column type refuses the response. The first live run may therefore end query-incomplete (exit 1, no zero claim). That fails closed. If it happens, file the observed envelope keys and meta types, without values, and the parser can be widened in a small follow-up. The withholding barrier matches the token's JSON-escaped form only, so a token containing a quote or backslash would not match. That is acceptable because no external string can enter the report.\n\nSimplification: acceptable, with one note. metricLocation slices the location function out of packages/scope/src/operational.ts, strips its types and evaluates it with Function(). It runs only checked-in source, never provider input. It is brittle, though: a rename or a new dependency in that function makes every report refuse. A small shared module for location would remove the evaluation. That is a candidate for the parent R14 work, not a gap here.\n\nVerdict: approved at exact head 1c89fa19. The builder lands. The landing closes child 00abd5aa only; parent R14 bb763efa and 0f0eeab7 stay open. No builder deployment; the planner runs the script with its own token and mapping and files the first report under R14.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:672f4e546dff7626bc779d7259553953ff0793f0","binding":"assigned","head":"1c89fa19d126e2eb3ed8f77b9430969442ed35f4","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:190f63c0cf2e4274b985abd820759cdc5f30b6ae\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:fe24c6795c1ccd36466004d6f37f057dd20a089a\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:190f63c0cf2e4274b985abd820759cdc5f30b6ae","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}